'''Kwinti''' is an [[English-based creole]] of [[Suriname]] closely related to [[Ndyuka language|Ndyuka]].{{sfn|Hoogbergen|1992|p=123}} The language has less than 300 speakers,{{sfn|Borges|2014|p=195}} and split from Plantation Creole which is nowadays known as [[Sranan Tongo]] in the middle 18th century.{{sfn|Borges|2014|p=188}} [[Code-switching]] with Sranan Tongo and [[Dutch language|Dutch]] was common among the younger generation in 1973,{{sfn|Elst|1973|p=14}} and about 70% of the tribe have moved to the urban areas.<ref>{{cite journal|title=The Maroon Population Explosion: Suriname and Guyane|series= New West Indian Guide / Nieuwe West-Indische Gids Volume 87: Issue 3-4|author=Richard Price|author-link=Richard Price (American anthropologist)|journal=New West Indian Guide|year=2013|volume=87|issue=3–4|pages=323–327|doi=10.1163/22134360-12340110|s2cid=140546216 |doi-access=free}}</ref> UNESCO considers the language endangered.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mona.uwi.edu/dllp/jlu/ciel/pages/kwinti.htm|title=Kwinti|website=The University of the West-Indies, Jamaica|access-date=29 July 2020|archive-date=29 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029171921/https://www.mona.uwi.edu/dllp/jlu/ciel/pages/kwinti.htm|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In the 1970s, Jan English-Lueck collected a vocabulary of 500 words. Unlike the Ndyuka languages, the letter ''r'' is spoken in a similar way to Sranan Tongo and Dutch, although speakers without ''r'' have been discovered later. About three quarters of the words were cognate to Sranan Tongo, very few (circa 3%) were cognate to [[Matawai language|Matawai]], and about 17% were not found in the other creoles and mainly originated from Dutch.{{sfn|Borges|2014|pp=188-189}} The differences can be explained by education, because according to a 2011 study the population of [[Witagron]] had a good command of both Dutch and Sranan Tongo.{{sfn|Borges|2014|p=191}}

